From 83867ed88f9441570ff12f608d0269c707b01d78 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: warelik <warelik@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 11:28:27 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 01/12] Add title_bar_to_status_bar setting

The title bar spends a whole row of vertical space on a few controls,
while the status bar at the bottom of the window has room for them.
title_bar_to_status_bar hides the title bar and shows its contents in
the status bar instead, which gives that row back to the editor. macOS
only: Windows and Linux draw their window controls in the title bar,
so the setting has no effect there.

On macOS the traffic lights move into the tab-height header beside
them and are centred between the window's top edge and the header's
bottom border, as far from the left edge as from the top. The button
size differs between macOS SDKs, so it is read from the live buttons
(Window::traffic_light_size). The position follows the header when its
height changes, including UI font size zoom, which bypasses settings.

The reservation under the buttons also covers zoomed items, whose
overlay is no longer below a title bar: a zoomed pane keeps the spacer
in its tab bar, a zoomed panel gets the same tab-height row as the
left dock, and the zoomed header starts at the window's top edge so
the buttons stay centred in it. A top-left pane without a tab bar
(tab_bar.show: false, or no items) gets the same tab-height row the
left dock uses, so the buttons never cover its toolbar or content.

The strips beside the traffic lights keep the title bar's drag and
double-click-to-zoom behaviour. The empty space of the tab bar only
drags the window: a double-click there keeps opening a new file
instead of zooming.

Deserialization in WorkspaceSettings::from_settings uses unwrap_or(false)
for title_bar_to_status_bar, ensuring that omitting the key or parsing
partial settings falls back cleanly to stock behavior without panicking.
